-- title:
--   Theorem 1.1 — nearly optimal expected insertion time
-- statement:
--   For every $\delta>0$ there exists $C_\delta>0$ such that the following holds for every prime table size $n$, every completion order fixed before sampling hashes, and every integer $0\le m<n$. Put $\varepsilon=1-m/n$ and suppose $1/\sqrt{\log n}<\varepsilon<1$. Starting from an empty table, insert $m$ distinct keys with independent uniform hashes and then an independently hashed next key. Its actual number $T_m$ of probes satisfies
--   $$\frac{1}{8\varepsilon}\le\mathbb E[T_m]\le C_\delta\varepsilon^{-(1+\delta)}.$$
--   The constant depends only on $\delta$, never on the prime, load, completion permutation or hash realization. This states the full exponent-one asymptotic as $\varepsilon^{-1}$ grows; no one exponent slack replaces its universal quantifier. It also gives $O(1)$ expected cost for every fixed admissible vacancy fraction, including $\varepsilon>1/2$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Every admissible prime is retained: sufficiently-large thresholds in supporting estimates are absorbed into $C_\delta$ using the deterministic $2n$ cost bound. The lower constant $1/8$ is a model consequence of the marginal vacancy probability and the elementary union bound; it makes the lower side of the headline exponent assertion explicit. Integer loads encode exactly the source's implicit integrality, with no rounding. The table distribution is generated by first-empty insertion, never supplied by association or tail certificates.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: m=(1−ε)n keys → integer m<n and ε=1−m/n; reason: exactly the attainable loads, without rounding m or requiring exact half-load
--   - encoding: distinct keys with independent uniform hashes and arbitrary fixed offset completion → insertion-time indices, ZMod n slots, every fixed completion permutation, and exact finite averages; reason: key labels do not affect insertion; repeated quadratic probes and the first empty slot are retained
--   - reading: expected time ε^{−(1+o(1))}, with ε⁻¹ the asymptotic parameter → ∀δ>0, ∃Cδ>0 uniform over all admissible primes and loads, and the universal lower bound 1/(8ε); reason: the proof gives the uniform upper side; the elementary model lower bound retains the exponent-one lower side required by the headline
-- source:
--   Yang S. Hu, William Kuszmaul, Jingxun Liang, Stefan Walzer, Huacheng Yu, Renfei Zhou, STOC 2027 submission #717 (October 2026), Theorem 1.1; restatement and proof, p. 15, p. 2 (PDF p. 2)

import Definitions.Def_QuadraticProbe717_Insertion_laws

set_option autoImplicit false

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter
open scoped NNReal

namespace QuadraticProbe717.Insertion

theorem expected_insertion_time :
    ∀ δ : ℝ, 0 < δ → ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧
      ∀ (n : ℕ) [NeZero n], n.Prime →
      ∀ (π : Equiv.Perm (ZMod n)) (m : ℕ), m < n →
      let ε := vacancyFraction n m
      1 / Real.sqrt (Real.log n) < ε → ε < 1 →
        1 / (8 * ε) ≤ expectedCost n m π ∧
        expectedCost n m π ≤ C * ε ^ (-(1 + δ)) := by sorry

end QuadraticProbe717.Insertion
